{
  "result": "I've always loved the places that are meant to be left behind. Train stations at 2 a.m., airport gates, 24-hour laundromats, the quiet aisle in a grocery store right before closing. They are not destinations. No one frames a photo of a waiting room and hangs it on their wall, but that's exactly why they are honest.\n\nIn these in-between spaces, people forget to perform. A businessman with a perfectly knotted tie lets his shoulders slump while he stares at the departure board. A teenager, headphones on, dances just a little too freely between the washers. A mother rocks a stroller, not for an audience, but because the motion is the only thing keeping her awake. Everyone is temporarily unassigned, existing without a role to play.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to arrive. We study to graduate, we work to get promoted, we save to finally travel. But life, I think, mostly happens in the transit. It's in the rehearsal, not the performance. The draft, not the book. The walk to the store where you notice the way light falls through the maple leaves and makes the sidewalk look like it's breathing.\n\nIf you ever feel lost, you're probably just in a hallway between two rooms. Don't rush. Look at the paintings on the walls. Listen to the hum of the lights. Hallways are part of the house too. And if you stay a moment longer, you might find you belong there, too, even for a while.",
